{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1089714-multiple-actions-a-simple-houserule","id":1089714,"title":"Multiple Actions - A simple houserule.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-08T05:10:00-07:00","id":1089714,"title":"Multiple Actions - A simple houserule.","content":"Grrrrreetings!<br /> <br /> Welcome to this thread, wherein I present a simple Multiple-Actions houserule that tries to fix a few logical inconsistencies and annoyances with the Multiple-Action rules as presented in Revised-, as well as the 2nd/20th Multiple-Action rules.<br /> <br /> First off, let's recap the rules-as-written:<br /> <br /> --<br /> <br /> Revised:<br /> <i>The first action gets a dice penalty of X dice, where X is the amount of actions you take. Each additional action gets a cumulative -1 penalty to the dicepool.</i><br /> I.e. in a three-action turn, the first gets -3 dice, the second -4, the third -5<br /> <br /> Vampire Dark Ages 20th Anniversary:<br /> <i>Each action, starting with the first, gets -1 cumulative penalty to the dicepool, and a cumulative +1 to difficulty.</i><br /> I.e. in a three-action turn, the first gets -1 dice/+1 diff, the second gets -2 dice/+2 diff, etc.<br /> <br /> 2nd Edition/All other 20th Editions:<br /> Take the lowest dice pool of all actions you plan to take in a turn. Split this dicepool among all actions you do.<br /> Example: Action A has a 6 dice pool, Action B has a 4 dice pool, action C has a 8 dice pool. You need to distribute 4 dice among action A, B and C for the turn.<br /> <br /> --<br /> <br /> Personally I think each of those rules has either inconsistencies in their logic, or is rather unbalanced.<br /> Here are the problems:<br /> <br /> Revised and VA: DA 20th rules.<br /> --------------------------------------------<br /> The two rules are very similiar, but both rules have, to me, the same logic problem:<br /> If you take three actions instead of one, then for simplicity's sake you only have one third of the time for each action than you normally would.<br /> So why, then, do those systems punish actions the harder, the later you take them in that turn? It stands to reason that each action should take an equal hit of difficulty instead. After all, each of them takes the same time penalty.<br /> <br /> 2nd Edition/Other 20th Edition Book rules:<br /> -------------------------------------------------------------<br /> The split-pool mechanics, in a way, circumvent the problem of later actions becoming harder - actually they let the player *decide* on how to distribute the difficulty by allowing them to give an action more, the other actions less dice.<br /> However, this rule actually has two problems:<br /> * The dice pool hit for all the actions across the board is *absolutely brutal* unless all actions you plan to take would normally have a high dice pool.<br /> * Automatic Actions do not change difficulties at all. You can shovel as many of them into the turn, and the penalties of actually rolled actions stay the same. In the Revised/VA: DA20 system(s), automatic actions *do* count towards the total actions you take - a realistic, balancing approach to me.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Thus, given all this, I present a simple houserule:<br /> <i>&quot; When taking X multiple actions a turn, all(!) actions that turn get a dice penalty equal to X. Actions fueled by supernatural powers like Celerity, Time Sphere, Rage etc. do not increase X, but are also subject to the penalty that turn. &quot;</i><br /> <br /> Example:<br /> Doing three actions in a turn gives them all a -3 dice penalty.<br /> Doing two actions, plus Rage/Time/Celerity actions gives *all* actions a -2 penalty. ( Two actions not fueled by supernatural means )<br /> Doing a normal action, plus Rage/Time/Celerity actions gives no penalty. As usual.<br /> <br /> Simple, no?<br /> This houserule does a couple of things:<br /> * All actions get an equal difficulty hit, depending purely on the amount of actions taken. This includes automatic actions, preventing abuse of those.<br /> * No actions get punished for being done later than others - again, a thing that makes sense to me, given that all actions get an equal time hit for simplicity.<br /> * Actions you are better at (i.e. have a larger dice pool for) get punished less than actions you are bad at - another thing that IMO makes sense.<br /> * Power-based extra actions get treated as usual, except that you can actually add multiple 'mundane' actions to the turn - with the penalty for that even affecting the power-based extra actions, however the overall penalty still is lower than what it normally would be.<br /> <br /> <br /> So, what do you think? Is it a good idea, or a bad one?<br /> Wether the penalty is &quot;X dice&quot;, &quot;X+1 dice&quot;, or &quot;X difficulty&quot; is up for grabs and personal adjustment of course, but anything like that still keeps the base principle of the houserule.<br /> <br /> <br /> -","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 05-08-2017, 08:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T05:49:09-07:00","id":1089728,"title":"","content":"There are thoughts behind all of these rules.<br /> Revised/V20: You take your normal action, but for every further action you have to speed up, making things more difficult as you can't keep up with everything you want to do.<br /> 2nd Ed/all other 20s: While you plan your time so you can do all those things, there's only a very limited time frame to actually do it. You try to do everything equaly good or prioritize one action, while the other one only gets limited attention. When trying to do something you're not quite sure about (low dice pool), that's a real limiter.<br /> <br /> Both of these approaches are quite valid, I don't think one is 'better' than the other.<br /> <br /> Your approach: Doing all things under the pressure of time, makes everything more difficult. It's also very reasonable. It would depend on what the storyteller thinks is best. Personally, I want to take a look on probability charts right now, to try and determine if one approach would be unbalanced in comparison. If not, one could even allow all of these rules, depending on how the player thinks his character would plan his move.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13221-thevarulfen","name":"TheVarulfen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T08:06:53-07:00","id":1089790,"title":"","content":"I think that the action economy is problematic for cWoD because it allows extra actions to overwhelm everything. You need to fix defense before you can really fix offense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T09:25:26-07:00","id":1089828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheVarulfen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1089714-multiple-actions-a-simple-houserule?p=1089728#post1089728\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If not, one could even allow all of these rules, depending on how the player thinks his character would plan his move.</div> </div> </div> </div>A valid and interesting point. I really wonder what statistics would have to say about which approach is the most, shall we say, advantageous.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1089714-multiple-actions-a-simple-houserule?p=1089790#post1089790\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that the action economy is problematic for cWoD because it allows extra actions to overwhelm everything. You need to fix defense before you can really fix offense.</div> </div> </div> </div>Multiple actions are not necessary about multiple attacks. As a matter of fact, V: DA20 explicitely has a rule that you *can't* do multiple attacks per turn unless you use something like Celerity.<br /> <br /> However, when it comes to combat I don't see an issue with multiple attacks, if the difficulty/dice modificer is tuned just right - it would pretty much mimic the RL difference between somebody going for a precise, heavy punch or throwing a barrage of lighter, less aimed punches. Maybe the stamina rating can actually be a limiting factor of punches per round in that regard. Additionally, the dice penalty also gets applied to the strength roll due to less time to focus the swing. Many ways to skin this cat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T10:24:27-07:00","id":1089850,"title":"","content":"I kind of like the flat penalty approach. I've been considering it for some time as other ST based systems have done similar things to simplify multiple actions if nothing else.<br /> <br /> However I think there's something of note:<br /> <br /> Multiple actions represent to distinct things that have two distinct sets of logic to them. Each of the extant multiple action rules really caters to one more than the other, but a compromise kinda caters to neither.<br /> <br /> Basically, a multiple action can be &quot;independent&quot; where the success/failure of each isn't inherently linked to each other (I shoot Alice once, and Bob once. Shooting Bob doesn't depend on how well I shoot Alice). These generally feel more &quot;right&quot; with the Rev/V20DA method where the penalties feel more like being there for switching gears. Second multiple actions can be &quot;chains&quot; where you're trying to do things that combo together (I run a significant distance, jump over a car, and kick Bob). You have to do one for the next to make sense. The old/new split technique makes this feel more right because it's best when you're good at everything so you can do all that, and having weak points in your plans can easily mess it up.<br /> <br /> Obviously application of those isn't 100% great in either.<br /> <br /> I feel it's worth considering two multiple action schemes. It can be confusing, but I think it allows greater narrative logic to balance out with better rules.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T04:31:22-07:00","id":1090230,"title":"","content":"Ehh I'm fine with just using Revised Multi Action rules, of your action plus 1. I really hated 20th taking such a stupid leap backwards...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T02:25:39-07:00","id":1090648,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1089714-multiple-actions-a-simple-houserule?p=1089790#post1089790\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that the action economy is problematic for cWoD because it allows extra actions to overwhelm everything. You need to fix defense before you can really fix offense.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Multiple attacks are generally only possible with multiple weapons, which have their own penalties to difficulty, or with celerity, time magic or some other supernatural ability that has some kind of draw (like Rage).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13221-thevarulfen","name":"TheVarulfen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T06:07:36-07:00","id":1090697,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheVarulfen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1089714-multiple-actions-a-simple-houserule?p=1090648#post1090648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Multiple attacks are generally only possible with multiple weapons, which have their own penalties to difficulty, or with celerity, time magic or some other supernatural ability that has some kind of draw (like Rage).</div> </div> </div> </div>Not quite. The only actual 20th anniversary book explicitely denying the use of the multiple action rules for multiple attacks, is the Vampire: Dark Ages 20th Edition one. It is also the only book that does not use the split-dicepools rule.<br /> I don't think those two combined things are a coincidence, as Revised/VA<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />: DA20 style multi-action rules would make multiple attacks way more powerful than the split-dice rule does. With the split-pools rule, multiple attacks get quite the dampener unless special powers are used.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T10:37:10-07:00","id":1090777,"title":"","content":"Dividing dice pools makes the most sense to me. You're cramming everything into three seconds. It's really not a lot of time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T14:11:40-07:00","id":1090865,"title":"","content":"Three seconds is, in this context, a lot of time. 3 seconds is enough time for the current record holder to empty a six round revolver into a single stationary target twice without missing. That is, fire six shots, reload, and fire six more, without missing a single shot on the target. Yes, real world you couldn't fire 12 times accurately because things are moving and such, but 3 seconds is a really long time in a system that tracks individual attacks. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T19:53:49-07:00","id":1090967,"title":"","content":"GURPS uses a one second turn, so the three second turn of cWoD is a rather luxurious pace. In GURPS, you can attempt to attack up to three times and attempt parry up to three attacks (as well as attempting to dodge a dozen attacks) during that one second without having a supernatural character. Of course, you are only moving one or two yards per second if you decide to attack and defend, but that is because we know what the maximum realistic speed for a human is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}